{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And it shall come to pass, because (עֵקֶב) you shall hearken, etc.</b> One needs to understand the verse's saying <b>וְהָיָה</b> — what shall come to pass? If the observance that it says immediately after is the meaning, it should have said only עֵקֶב תִּשְׁמְעוּן וְשָׁמַר — and what need is there for the word וְהָיָה? Further, one needs to understand the verse's saying <b>עֵקֶב</b> and not saying 'if you hearken' in the manner of its saying (Leviticus 26:3) ״אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי״. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Tanchuma): if the commandments that a person tramples with his heels (בַּעֲקֵבָיו) you hearken — and this is the way of derash." "And it may be explained on the way of their saying of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 42:3): וְהָיָה means nothing but joy. Now, the master of the prophets came with the pleasantness of his words to awaken with pleasant discipline, that a person has no right to rejoice unless he observes to do all that HaShem has commanded to do — then his heart rejoices and his glory exults. But as long as he feels that he lacks one of all the commandments of HaShem, whether negative commandments or positive commandments, concerning him Solomon said (Ecclesiastes 2): ״וּלְשִׂמְחָה מַה זֹּה עוֹשָׂה״. And that is the meaning of <b>וְהָיָה</b> — joy shall be yours — <b>עֵקֶב תִּשְׁמְעוּן</b>; meaning, עֵקֶב is the end and consummation, in the manner that we find our Sages of blessed memory using this term in the language of the Mishnah (Sotah 49b): בְּעִקְּבוֹת מְשִׁיחָא, etc., for at the completion of the time of his (the Messiah's) coming it is called עִקְּבוֹת. So too it says <b>עֵקֶב תִּשְׁמְעוּן אֶת הַמִּשְׁפָּטִים וּשְׁמַרְתֶּם וַעֲשִׂיתֶם</b> — then is the time of joy, and whoever has not reached this has no place to rejoice. As the saying of the pious of Israel (Chovot HaLevavot, Sha'ar HaPerishut, ch. 4) goes, that the meaning is: mourning in his heart and brightness in his face, for it is not fitting for one who is destined to stand in shame and disgrace before the great King to rejoice — and needless to say one whose life is forfeit to the King. According to this, the measure of עֵקֶב is the completion of hearing all the mishpatim." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to say that a person has no joy except at the end of the hearing, and as long as a person has not finished his service he should not trust in the righteousness of his deeds, in the manner of the verse (Job 15:15): ״הֵן בִּקְדוֹשָׁיו לֹא יַאֲמִין״." "<b>Further,</b> it intends that one must study Torah with joy and not with sadness, for words of Torah are forbidden to a mourner (Yoreh De'ah, siman 384)." "<b>Further,</b> he alluded on the way of the verse (Psalms 19:9) ״פִּקּוּדֵי ה׳ יְשָׁרִים מְשַׂמְּחֵי לֵב״ — and that is the meaning of <b>וְהָיָה</b>: the joy shall be <b>עֵקֶב תִּשְׁמְעוּן</b>, and the meaning of עֵקֶב — the reward of the labor of Torah will bring him joy. And this is what the Tanna alluded in his saying (Avot 4:2): מִצְוָה גּוֹרֶרֶת מִצְוָה, etc. — that the reward of a mitzvah is a mitzvah, which is the joy." "<b>Further,</b> he alluded to what they said in the Zohar (Zohar vol. 3, p. 113), and this is its language: there is no joy before the Holy One, blessed be He, like the hour when Israel exerts itself in the Torah. And this is its saying <b>וְהָיָה</b> absolutely — comprehensive joy to fill the world; and when He is joyful, all the world is in joy and gladness ״עֵקֶב תִּשְׁמְעוּן״ etc. And in the word <b>עֵקֶב</b> he alluded to the quality of Torah, that a person should make himself walk as a heel alongside the great, in humility and lowliness — and then he will succeed in hearing the studies. And this is its saying <b>תִּשְׁמְעוּן</b>, also in the passive conjugation of being heard, to say that through the device by which a person makes himself like heels, the secrets of Torah will be revealed to him and he will understand them." "<b>And it is possible</b> that he alluded, in the letter nun, that he will attain and understand fifty gates of understanding. And according to this approach, the grammatical form of the verse is <b>תִּשְׁמְעוּן</b> — that if he attains the level of humility, he will attain hearing the words of Torah. And its saying <b>וּשְׁמַרְתֶּם</b> etc. means, that through comprehension in the Torah he will attain to keep and to do — for the Torah protects and shields from sin, which is the keeping (shemirah), and also brings about fulfillment of the commandments, which is the deed (ma'aseh)." "<b>And He kept etc.</b> It said it with an added vav, and according to the plain sense of the verse it is the imperative form, and it should have said ״יִשְׁמֹר ה׳״ etc. But according to what I explained in the preceding verse according to one of the approaches, that the root-form is ״עֵקֶב,״ and according to one of the approaches the root-form is ״תִּשְׁמְעוּן,״ the saying of <b>וְשָׁמַר</b> with an added vav will come out correctly." "<b>to you the covenant etc.</b> It is difficult, for from his words here it implies that after they hear and keep and do all the statutes, through this they will merit that He will keep His promise to them. Yet from what he says in this very parashah (Deuteronomy 9:5) ״לֹא בְצִדְקָתְךָ״ etc. ״כִּי בְּרִשְׁעַת״ etc. ״וּלְמַעַן הָקִים״ etc. — this indicates that even though they have no merit, HaShem must fulfill the oath. And this is your sign, that it was not by his righteousness that he came and inherited what he inherited. Or perhaps, for their entry into the land was on account of the oath to the fathers, and this is a condition — if they keep and do, HaShem will keep the gift forever and ever, and this is its saying here <b>וְשָׁמַר ה׳</b>; and if they do not keep, the fulfillment of the oath will be only for the brief time that they entered the land, and then He will send them off on their own." "<b>Further,</b> it may be explained along this line: for regarding the promise to Abraham, we find that He made him two promises: (1) to bring them out of Egypt and bring them into the land of Canaan, as it is written (Genesis 15:14) ״וְאַחֲרֵי כֵן יֵצְאוּ בִּרְכֻשׁ גָּדוֹל וְדוֹר רְבִיעִי יָשׁוּבוּ הֵנָּה״; (2) to give him from the river of Egypt to the river Euphrates and ten nations. Now, even though they were not as righteous as required, as it says ״לֹא בְצִדְקָתְךָ״ etc., nevertheless HaShem fulfilled for their children the first promise, that He brought them out of Egypt and brought them into the land and bequeathed to them a sufficient measure. Yet you will find that even the measure they needed they did not inherit, for the children of Joseph stand and cry out that their inheritance did not suffice them (Joshua 17:14) — see, the promise of from the river of Egypt to the river Euphrates and ten nations has still not reached their hands. Therefore Moses said <b>וְהָיָה עֵקֶב תִּשְׁמְעוּן</b> etc. <b>וְשָׁמַר ה׳</b> etc. <b>אֶת הַבְּרִית</b> etc. — meaning, to give from the river of Egypt and ten nations in fullness." "<b>Or</b> it may be said that concerning the seven nations He says <b>et the covenant</b>, and concerning the completion of the ten He says <b>and et the kindness</b>, for the gift of the three is extraordinary kindness since they are besides the land of Canaan. And the one who understands the matter will see that they comprise most of the world, for Edom, Ammon, and Moab are a great corner, and all the more so according to their words, may their memory be for blessing (Yoma 10a), that they said: the kingdom of Edom is destined to spread throughout the entire world. From this it follows that this gift includes the entire world within it, and it will be called extraordinary kindness, and concerning that He says ״וְאֶת הַחֶסֶד״. And you shall know that this is so, for Israel has not yet merited the aforementioned promise, and that is what we ask and hope for — that the Supernal One bequeath to us many nations." "<b>Further</b>, it may intend along this line: <b>And it shall come to pass, because you heed</b>, etc. — you will not need to merit the goodness of HaShem and His kindness by virtue of the oath to the fathers, but rather you will merit on your own account — to observe for you the covenant, etc., that He swore to your fathers — for one who merits on his own account is not comparable to one who merits on account of his fathers. And this is what is precisely stated by saying <b>to you et the covenant</b>, and He did not say ״וְשָׁמַר לְךָ ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ etc., for the intent was to join the word ״לְךָ״ with the word ״הַבְּרִית״ to alert you to our words. And along this line the verse says <b>and He will observe</b> — meaning, all the promises of HaShem, and needless to say His covenants, are guaranteed and preserved forever and ever. And these matters are true, for did not Abraham merit all the honor on account of his deeds? And if Israel observe the Torah of HaShem, why should they be diminished from meriting through the power of the merit of the fathers? And on the contrary — theirs is greater than that of their fathers, for they stood at Mount Sinai, and also the very fact that they were commanded in the Torah and the commandments, which was not so for the fathers, to whom the Torah was not given. From this it follows that the power of the children, when their deeds are good, is greater than the power of the fathers."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {3 "The Hebrew citation 'שער הפרישות פרק ד׳' in comment 1 is correctly rendered as Sha'ar HaPerishut chapter 4. Here, the printed text reads 'סימן שפד' (= 284), but the standard location for laws of Torah study by mourners is Yoreh De'ah siman 384 (שפד in Hebrew letters may be a typographical variant or error for שפד = 384 is actually שפד = 284; the standard reference is 384). Translated the citation as given.", 8 "The word ה׳ in the phrase ״יִשְׁמֹר ה׳״ is rendered HaShem per the divine-names rule; here HaShem is the subject of ישמור, not a prefix to the verb."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:16:34.804653162Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}